00:10:17.960 --> 00:10:21.440
get it. That's totally fine. I do it

00:10:19.840 --> 00:10:22.920
myself, you know, I have a fairly nice

00:10:21.440 --> 00:10:25.160
set now. I have a fairly nice camera

00:10:22.920 --> 00:10:26.880
now. But, just know that that doesn't

00:10:25.160 --> 00:10:28.360
make that much of a difference. That's

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:30.440
more of a brand play just to make your

00:10:28.360 --> 00:10:32.160
brand stand out from other people's.

00:10:30.440 --> 00:10:33.360
But, when you're first growing, that's

00:10:32.160 --> 00:10:36.080
not what you should do. That's

00:10:33.360 --> 00:10:37.880
optimization. want to optimize something

00:10:36.080 --> 00:10:39.520
that hasn't even proven that it works

00:10:37.880 --> 00:10:41.160
yet. That's like polishing a turd.

00:10:39.520 --> 00:10:43.480
That's like putting salt and pepper on a

00:10:41.160 --> 00:10:46.080
rotten egg. It's not going to help. And

00:10:43.480 --> 00:10:48.120
this brings me to the next point. This

00:10:46.080 --> 00:10:52.680
is the third strategy, what I like to

00:10:48.120 --> 00:10:55.640
call yap maxing, okay? So, this is

00:10:52.680 --> 00:10:58.240
basically where conversations that you

00:10:55.640 --> 00:11:00.400
would have had with friends, uh

00:10:58.240 --> 00:11:03.960
entrepreneur buddies, people in your

00:11:00.400 --> 00:11:06.960
company, etc., you have these types of

00:11:03.960 --> 00:11:09.600
conversations on camera, okay? So, you

00:11:06.960 --> 00:11:11.839
do these on camera instead of doing them

00:11:09.600 --> 00:11:14.880
in private. Now, one thing I'll say is

00:11:11.839 --> 00:11:16.640
it's probably very easy if somebody on

00:11:14.880 --> 00:11:17.920
your team asks you a question, right? If

00:11:16.640 --> 00:11:19.480
somebody on your team asks you a

00:11:17.920 --> 00:11:20.760
question, and it's, you know, maybe a

00:11:19.480 --> 00:11:22.920
question that came from a client, or

00:11:20.760 --> 00:11:24.720
maybe a client on a call, on a Zoom

00:11:22.920 --> 00:11:26.920
call, or a phone call, etc., asked you a

00:11:24.720 --> 00:11:28.760
question, or maybe one of your friends

00:11:26.920 --> 00:11:30.240
asked you a question, and then, you

00:11:28.760 --> 00:11:33.560
know, you send a Loom video to to

00:11:30.240 --> 00:11:35.440
answering it. It's super easy to talk,

00:11:33.560 --> 00:11:36.960
right? You could do it all day long. In

00:11:35.440 --> 00:11:38.080
fact, you know, you might be at a dinner

00:11:36.960 --> 00:11:40.360
party, you might be hanging out with

00:11:38.080 --> 00:11:42.440
friends, and you can just talk for 12

00:11:40.360 --> 00:11:43.760
hours straight. There's certain friends

00:11:42.440 --> 00:11:45.920
you can just talk to all day long, and

00:11:43.760 --> 00:11:47.840
you don't get tired. But, for some

00:11:45.920 --> 00:11:50.760
reason, when it comes to recording

00:11:47.840 --> 00:11:53.680
videos, you get fatigued. You get tired

00:11:50.760 --> 00:11:56.480
very quickly. Why is this? Well, I'm

00:11:53.680 --> 00:11:59.840
telling you right now that most of it is

00:11:56.480 --> 00:12:02.520
mental, okay? Most of it is all in your

00:11:59.840 --> 00:12:06.120
head. It's 90%

00:12:02.520 --> 00:12:08.560
worrying about doing the thing and 10%

00:12:06.120 --> 00:12:11.680
of the actual thing that's causing you

00:12:08.560 --> 00:12:13.560
to be tired, right? For some reason, you

00:12:11.680 --> 00:12:15.120
worry about how you're saying something

00:12:13.560 --> 00:12:17.840
or how you're coming off or you're not

00:12:15.120 --> 00:12:20.480
saying the perfect words or something

00:12:17.840 --> 00:12:21.920
along those lines. Whereas, if, you

00:12:20.480 --> 00:12:23.640
know, again, one of your friends asked

00:12:21.920 --> 00:12:25.080
you or one of your clients or, you know,

00:12:23.640 --> 00:12:26.400
somebody at your company, etc., asked

00:12:25.080 --> 00:12:28.720
you the same exact question, you would

00:12:26.400 --> 00:12:30.320
be able to just fluently answer them.

00:12:28.720 --> 00:12:32.040
But, the fact that there's a camera on

00:12:30.320 --> 00:12:33.600
you makes you a little bit nervous, and

00:12:32.040 --> 00:12:35.160
you're not able to answer nearly as

00:12:33.600 --> 00:12:36.600
well. And this is really fatiguing,

00:12:35.160 --> 00:12:38.760
right? It really makes you tired after a

00:12:36.600 --> 00:12:42.040
while. And again, remember back to this

00:12:38.760 --> 00:12:43.800
analogy. If you were just talking to a

00:12:42.040 --> 00:12:45.480
good friend of yours who, you know, you

00:12:43.800 --> 00:12:46.880
talk about business with, like an

00:12:45.480 --> 00:12:48.560
entrepreneur buddy or maybe somebody on

00:12:46.880 --> 00:12:50.000
your team or a friend or whatever, you

00:12:48.560 --> 00:12:51.000
could easily talk to them for like 12

00:12:50.000 --> 00:12:52.320
hours straight. You could talk to them

00:12:51.000 --> 00:12:53.880
all day long, and you wouldn't get

00:12:52.320 --> 00:12:56.520
tired. So, why is it that you get tired

00:12:53.880 --> 00:12:59.560
after 30 minutes of recording? This This

00:12:56.520 --> 00:13:01.960
is why. It's all mental. And this is why

00:12:59.560 --> 00:13:03.440
as much as possible, you should try to

00:13:01.960 --> 00:13:04.839
record stuff live. So, these

00:13:03.440 --> 00:13:06.280
conversations that you'd be having with

00:13:04.839 --> 00:13:08.320
your friends or other entrepreneurs or

00:13:06.280 --> 00:13:10.400
people in your company, etc., try to

00:13:08.320 --> 00:13:11.839
record it live. This is what Hormozi's

00:13:10.400 --> 00:13:13.680
doing right now, for instance. Almost

00:13:11.839 --> 00:13:15.480
all of his content on his channel, he

00:13:13.680 --> 00:13:17.120
actually just records it live in front

00:13:15.480 --> 00:13:18.480
of a live audience, and that's what

00:13:17.120 --> 00:13:20.280
makes it so much easier for him to

00:13:18.480 --> 00:13:22.240
actually present the information. Now,

00:13:20.280 --> 00:13:24.040
if for whatever reason, let's just say

00:13:22.240 --> 00:13:24.839
you're in a Zoom call with your team,

00:13:24.040 --> 00:13:26.760
there's a few things that are

00:13:24.839 --> 00:13:28.440
confidential, you're not able to really

00:13:26.760 --> 00:13:31.040
publish that and make it live, that's

00:13:28.440 --> 00:13:34.400
totally fine. So, what you do instead is

00:13:31.040 --> 00:13:36.880
I like to call that a yap session, okay?

00:13:34.400 --> 00:13:38.960
So, I call it a yap session. And when I

00:13:36.880 --> 00:13:40.480
realized that I'm dropping something

00:13:38.960 --> 00:13:42.520
that would be really valuable for other

00:13:40.480 --> 00:13:43.960
people to hear, I will take that clip

00:13:42.520 --> 00:13:45.440
and it's really easy to get that if

00:13:43.960 --> 00:13:47.000
you're just using something like Fathom

00:13:45.440 --> 00:13:48.760
for instance that automatically records

00:13:47.000 --> 00:13:51.280
your Zoom calls, but I will take that

00:13:48.760 --> 00:13:53.560
clip and then I will put it into AI. And

00:13:51.280 --> 00:13:55.320
then I'll have AI summarize all the most

00:13:53.560 --> 00:13:57.120
important points and make a script for

00:13:55.320 --> 00:13:58.800
me and an outline. And then I'll go

00:13:57.120 --> 00:14:00.280
ahead and go record that again. And

00:13:58.800 --> 00:14:01.640
usually I can remember most of it off

00:14:00.280 --> 00:14:03.120
the top of my head because I already

00:14:01.640 --> 00:14:04.560
said it. So, it's not that hard to

00:14:03.120 --> 00:14:06.480
actually just say it again, but we'll

00:14:04.560 --> 00:14:07.640
have AI go ahead and make a script. And

00:14:06.480 --> 00:14:08.880
of course, if you have the right systems

00:14:07.640 --> 00:14:10.640
and processes, you can just make the

00:14:08.880 --> 00:14:12.560
script a little bit better. And that is

00:14:10.640 --> 00:14:14.920
how it's done. So, one other thing I'll

00:14:12.560 --> 00:14:16.840
say about this is for a lot of our

00:14:14.920 --> 00:14:19.400
extremely busy clients, the way they

00:14:16.840 --> 00:14:22.880
make content is either one time per

00:14:19.400 --> 00:14:24.520
month or two times per month, like once

00:14:22.880 --> 00:14:26.320
every two weeks, they will get

00:14:24.520 --> 00:14:28.080
interviewed, right? So, they'll get

00:14:26.320 --> 00:14:30.240
interviewed and usually about a 1-hour

00:14:28.080 --> 00:14:32.880
session or so, and people will literally

00:14:30.240 --> 00:14:34.920
just ask them questions, okay? They'll

00:14:32.880 --> 00:14:37.680
ask them questions and then the person

00:14:34.920 --> 00:14:40.240
will answer those questions. That is

00:14:37.680 --> 00:14:42.200
then either made into content, depending

00:14:40.240 --> 00:14:45.640
on what they want to do, depending on

00:14:42.200 --> 00:14:47.240
what their setup is, or it's used as a

00:14:45.640 --> 00:14:50.960
yap session,

00:14:47.240 --> 00:14:50.960
turned into a script,

00:14:51.440 --> 00:14:55.800
and then recorded

00:14:54.000 --> 00:14:58.440
later on.

00:14:55.800 --> 00:15:00.760
So, again, the power of yap maxing, the

00:14:58.440 --> 00:15:02.600
power of just talking and just answering

00:15:00.760 --> 00:15:04.280
questions and pretending like you're not

00:15:02.600 --> 00:15:06.040
being recorded on camera and then just

00:15:04.280 --> 00:15:07.920
uploading that stuff to YouTube is so

00:15:06.040 --> 00:15:09.760
powerful. I could honestly go on and on

00:15:07.920 --> 00:15:12.920
about this as well because the truth is

00:15:09.760 --> 00:15:14.640
it comes across so much better as well

00:15:12.920 --> 00:15:16.320
when you're just saying stuff naturally,

00:15:14.640 --> 00:15:17.720
right? If you're using a teleprompter,

00:15:16.320 --> 00:15:19.000
which don't get me wrong, I don't have

00:15:17.720 --> 00:15:20.120
any problem with a teleprompter. I have

00:15:19.000 --> 00:15:21.520
one in front of me right now. I'm not

00:15:20.120 --> 00:15:23.280
really using it, but I don't have any

00:15:21.520 --> 00:15:24.440
problem with a teleprompter. But most

00:15:23.280 --> 00:15:26.680
people when they read off of a

00:15:24.440 --> 00:15:28.240
teleprompter, it's quite obvious that

00:15:26.680 --> 00:15:29.800
they're doing it, right? Even if you're

00:15:28.240 --> 00:15:32.120
pretty good at it, you've you know,

00:15:29.800 --> 00:15:33.880
practiced a lot, it's still relatively

00:15:32.120 --> 00:15:35.320
easy for other people to tell that

00:15:33.880 --> 00:15:37.800
you're reading off of a teleprompter.

00:15:35.320 --> 00:15:40.080
However, if you do the yap maxing, it

00:15:37.800 --> 00:15:43.600
comes across so much more natural and

00:15:40.080 --> 00:15:45.400
this builds more

00:15:43.600 --> 00:15:48.800
Guess what it is?

00:15:45.400 --> 00:15:49.640
Trust. It builds more trust. It makes

00:15:48.800 --> 00:15:51.720
you

00:15:49.640 --> 00:15:53.480
seem more authoritative.

00:15:51.720 --> 00:15:55.680
Uh it makes you seem more authentic,

00:15:53.480 --> 00:15:57.560
etc., etc. And this is a subject that I

00:15:55.680 --> 00:15:59.000
could go extremely deep on, but I think

00:15:57.560 --> 00:16:03.600
you get the point. All right, so the

00:15:59.000 --> 00:16:05.560
next strategy is to go deep.

00:16:03.600 --> 00:16:07.880
First,

00:16:05.560 --> 00:16:10.240
then

00:16:07.880 --> 00:16:13.680
go wide.

00:16:10.240 --> 00:16:16.480
Okay? And what I mean by this is you

00:16:13.680 --> 00:16:18.600
want to start

00:16:16.480 --> 00:16:21.560
niche. Okay? You want to start by

00:16:18.600 --> 00:16:23.240
helping a very specific client

00:16:21.560 --> 00:16:24.760
demographic. And what I always like to

00:16:23.240 --> 00:16:26.960
say when we're doing this is you either

00:16:24.760 --> 00:16:28.280
help your ideal client, so the person

00:16:26.960 --> 00:16:29.640
who is just easiest for you to get

00:16:28.280 --> 00:16:31.240
results for, or you want to help

00:16:29.640 --> 00:16:32.680
somebody who's the easiest to sell and

00:16:31.240 --> 00:16:34.160
the easiest person to generate the most

00:16:32.680 --> 00:16:35.920
revenue. So, it really depends on if

00:16:34.160 --> 00:16:38.080
you're optimizing for making money up

00:16:35.920 --> 00:16:39.160
front, aka the easiest person to sell or

00:16:38.080 --> 00:16:41.160
the person who generates the most

00:16:39.160 --> 00:16:43.520
revenue, or if you're optimizing for

00:16:41.160 --> 00:16:45.080
LTV, right? Optimizing for the person

00:16:43.520 --> 00:16:46.880
who's going to end up making you the

00:16:45.080 --> 00:16:48.440
most money in the long run. Because if

00:16:46.880 --> 00:16:49.720
they're really easy to get results for,

00:16:48.440 --> 00:16:51.160
you're going to get them results, you're

00:16:49.720 --> 00:16:52.200
going to get lots of testimonials,

00:16:51.160 --> 00:16:53.920
that's going to get you more clients,

00:16:52.200 --> 00:16:56.400
etc., etc. It's kind of like a snowball

00:16:53.920 --> 00:16:59.000
effect. So, what I always say is you

00:16:56.400 --> 00:17:00.680
really want to get this statement down.

00:16:59.000 --> 00:17:02.600
I help X

00:17:00.680 --> 00:17:07.720
do Y

00:17:02.600 --> 00:17:10.240
by Z. Okay? X is the market.

00:17:07.720 --> 00:17:14.240
Y is the problem they have, so it's

00:17:10.240 --> 00:17:16.040
either do Y or overcome Y. And Z is your

00:17:14.240 --> 00:17:19.000
solution.

00:17:16.040 --> 00:17:22.280
Okay? So, I help X do Y by Z. This is

00:17:19.000 --> 00:17:23.760
the niche hypothesis statement, okay?

00:17:22.280 --> 00:17:25.120
That's what I call it, niche hypothesis

00:17:23.760 --> 00:17:27.640
statement. And you really want to stick

00:17:25.120 --> 00:17:30.760
with this at first. So, the example of

00:17:27.640 --> 00:17:33.760
this is I mean, you can look at almost

00:17:30.760 --> 00:17:35.720
any successful YouTuber. Basically, what

00:17:33.760 --> 00:17:38.840
you want to do is you want to be the

00:17:35.720 --> 00:17:41.440
king of a puddle, then the king of a

00:17:38.840 --> 00:17:43.200
pond, then a lake, then a sea, then the

00:17:41.440 --> 00:17:45.040
ocean. You want to do it in that order.

00:17:43.200 --> 00:17:46.280
Be the king of a puddle. Be the best at

00:17:45.040 --> 00:17:48.240
serving someone in a very specific

00:17:46.280 --> 00:17:50.080
niche, then a pond, then a lake, then a

00:17:48.240 --> 00:17:52.160
sea, then the ocean. And there's so many

00:17:50.080 --> 00:17:53.840
reasons to start niche as well. I mean,

00:17:52.160 --> 00:17:56.440
I could go on and on about this, but one

00:17:53.840 --> 00:17:58.240
of the biggest ones is it's much easier

00:17:56.440 --> 00:18:00.200
for you to establish your authority when

00:17:58.240 --> 00:18:02.200
you start niche. Just as an example,

00:18:00.200 --> 00:18:04.400
when Alex Hormozi first started, he was

00:18:02.200 --> 00:18:06.160
helping gym owners with their sales and

00:18:04.400 --> 00:18:08.080
their marketing. AKA, helped gym owners

00:18:06.160 --> 00:18:09.800
get more clients, right? And it was very

00:18:08.080 --> 00:18:11.720
easy for him to establish his authority

00:18:09.800 --> 00:18:13.640
there because he'd had so much success

00:18:11.720 --> 00:18:16.240
helping gym owners get more clients. But

00:18:13.640 --> 00:18:17.320
then, he expanded to mortar businesses,

00:18:16.240 --> 00:18:18.640
and then to all different types of

00:18:17.320 --> 00:18:20.400
service businesses, whether they were

00:18:18.640 --> 00:18:21.960
online or not. And now, he basically

00:18:20.400 --> 00:18:23.400
just serves all different types of

00:18:21.960 --> 00:18:25.600
businesses in the world. But it would

00:18:23.400 --> 00:18:27.840
have been exceptionally difficult if he

00:18:25.600 --> 00:18:30.320
tried to do that at first. Starting off

00:18:27.840 --> 00:18:32.760
as the king of a puddle is the most

00:18:30.320 --> 00:18:35.880
important thing. Go deep first, then go

00:18:32.760 --> 00:18:38.640
wide. And this brings us into the next

00:18:35.880 --> 00:18:41.400
strategy, which is number five,

00:18:38.640 --> 00:18:43.600
optimize. Not for getting the most

00:18:41.400 --> 00:18:46.720
views, okay? Optimize for getting the

00:18:43.600 --> 00:18:50.400
most views? No. Not the most views.

00:18:46.720 --> 00:18:52.480
Optimize for getting the right views,

00:18:50.400 --> 00:18:55.760
right? That's what you want to do. So

00:18:52.480 --> 00:18:58.360
again, if you get 1 million views and

00:18:55.760 --> 00:19:00.920
you have zero potential clients, that is

00:18:58.360 --> 00:19:04.560
not nearly as good as getting 1,000

00:19:00.920 --> 00:19:07.360
views and having 50 potential clients.

00:19:04.560 --> 00:19:11.960
But guess what? That's not as good as

00:19:07.360 --> 00:19:14.320
getting 100,000 views and having 500

00:19:11.960 --> 00:19:17.320
potential clients. So, obviously, you

00:19:14.320 --> 00:19:19.800
want to optimize for the right views,

00:19:17.320 --> 00:19:21.760
right? These people, getting as many of

00:19:19.800 --> 00:19:22.960
these people as possible who could

00:19:21.760 --> 00:19:24.920
potentially be clients, right? They're

00:19:22.960 --> 00:19:27.000
really good prospects. That's what you

00:19:24.920 --> 00:19:28.800
want to optimize for. So, if that means

00:19:27.000 --> 00:19:30.640
getting 100,000 views per video, then

00:19:28.800 --> 00:19:32.280
get 100,000 views per video. If that

00:19:30.640 --> 00:19:34.200
means getting 1,000 views per video,

00:19:32.280 --> 00:19:36.000
then get 1,000 views per video. And in

00:19:34.200 --> 00:19:37.480
very rare cases, if that means getting a

00:19:36.000 --> 00:19:38.640
million views per video, then get a

00:19:37.480 --> 00:19:40.640
million views per video, but I can

00:19:38.640 --> 00:19:42.960
almost promise you that is very rare.

00:19:40.640 --> 00:19:45.160
Usually, the videos that do the best are

00:19:42.960 --> 00:19:46.800
going to be videos that get low views or

00:19:45.160 --> 00:19:48.200
medium amount of views. If you don't

00:19:46.800 --> 00:19:50.680
believe me, just look at what Alex

00:19:48.200 --> 00:19:52.480
Hormozi recently said in this video when

00:19:50.680 --> 00:19:53.680
talking about his highlights channel. He

00:19:52.480 --> 00:19:55.480
talked about how there was more

00:19:53.680 --> 00:19:57.760
attributable revenue coming from this

00:19:55.480 --> 00:19:59.960
highlights channel that has about 72,000

00:19:57.760 --> 00:20:03.120
subscribers than his main channel that

00:19:59.960 --> 00:20:05.280
has over 4 million subscribers. So, this

00:20:03.120 --> 00:20:08.200
highlights channel that gets like 1,000

00:20:05.280 --> 00:20:10.520
views per video or 1.9k views per video

00:20:08.200 --> 00:20:12.760
or, you know, 16,000

00:20:10.520 --> 00:20:15.280
is getting more attributable revenue

00:20:12.760 --> 00:20:18.160
than his main channel that gets millions

00:20:15.280 --> 00:20:22.800
of views, right? 37,000, 40,000,

00:20:18.160 --> 00:20:24.760
150,000, 125,000, 408,000, 641,000. And

00:20:22.800 --> 00:20:26.800
he's had many videos on this channel

00:20:24.760 --> 00:20:28.320
that have gotten millions of views. What

00:20:26.800 --> 00:20:30.320
does that tell you? Well, it's not about

00:20:28.320 --> 00:20:32.640
how many views you get, it's how many of

